WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.283
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.283 --> 00:00:07.200
I'm Saad Omer.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.030
I'm the director of the Yale
Institute for Global Health.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:11.760
I'm really delighted to welcome you

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.600
to the "Global Health Conversation" series

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.700
in addition to the Yale Institute
for Global Health, or YIGH.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.660
The series is co-sponsored
by the Walker Fund

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.393
at the MacMillan Center at Yale.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:23.800
Well, a lot of you probably know

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.040
that the eradication of disease

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.110
is defined as a deliberate set of efforts

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:31.930
towards a permanent reduction to zero

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:33.920
of the worldwide incidence of infection

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.140
caused by a specific agent.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.990
The eradication efforts require
enormous planning and effort

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:42.233
and implementation, and dedication.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.010
The eradication of smallpox,

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.950
the only human disease that
has been eradicated so far,

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.910
was spearheaded by the
Smallpox Eradication Program,

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:59.640
launched by the World
Health Organization in 1976.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:04.640
This multiprong initiative
focused on endemic countries,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.360
and utilized a combination
of surveillance,

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.830
case finding, contact
tracing, ring vaccination,

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.233
and communication campaigns,
to inform affected population.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:19.840
Fueled by worldwide support by 1973,

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:23.820
the number of countries where the smallpox

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.720
had substantially decreased,

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.350
with the last infection
reported in Somalia

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:32.583
on October 26th, 1977.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.480
So WHO teams searched for
possible smallpox infections

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:40.440
in the next couple of years,
finding no further cases.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:45.400
And at the 33rd World
Health Assembly in 1980,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.290
so approximately 42 years ago,

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:54.053
smallpox was officially deemed eradicated.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.740
To reflect this momentous effort

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:01.450
and discuss eradication in the context

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:04.940
of the present and the future,

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:09.370
I'm so pleased to have
with us, Dr. William Foege,

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.620
or Dr. Bill Foege, the former Chief

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:14.720
for Smallpox Eradication Program,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.830
and a former Director of the
CDC between 1977 and 1983.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:23.650
In his leadership role within

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:26.590
the Smallpox Eradication Program,

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.290
Dr. Foege pioneered the technique known as

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:33.850
the "Surveillance Containment,"
developed a combat,

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:37.130
spread of smallpox in the
face of vaccine shortages.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:38.900
So it was--

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.290
Public health is about
life giving you lemons

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.590
and making lemonade, that
was one useful lemonade

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.440
that helped us eradicate.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.590
And this was a combination of
both theoretical understanding

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.490
of what was going on, but
also applied public health.

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:57.120
And I remain as an infectious
disease epidemiologist,,

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.520
I remain a fan of that approach

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.833
that was very suitable for that disease.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.980
And so using this method,
Dr. Foege and his team

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:11.370
was able to play a major
role in eradicating

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:15.053
an infectious disease for the
first time in human history.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:19.060
Subsequently, and that was just--

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.450
I think it's reasonable to say now,

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.300
because we know the rest of the story,

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:26.223
that was the start of
his career, if you will,

00:03:26.223 --> 00:03:29.260
if you look at the full trajectory.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:34.260
He then went on to be a
legendary CDC director in 77',

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.970
where he served for six years.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:41.390
He co-founded the Task
Force for Child Survival

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:42.223
and Development,

00:03:42.223 --> 00:03:45.640
now known as "Task Force
for Global Health,"

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.140
played a major role in the development

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.893
and evolution of the Carter Center,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.000
provided invaluable
advice at the beginning

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.530
and the foundation of
the Gates Foundation,

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:57.820
and so on and so forth.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:01.810
He remains a Professor Emeritus at Emory.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:03.840
And personally speaking,

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.900
one of the proudest things in
my career, for me personally,

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:12.470
was to be the second ever
Bill Foege professor at Emory.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.360
And so that was an incredible honor.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.310
And as an aside, when
I would teach my class,

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:21.110
and Dr. Foege would lecture
in that on smallpox,

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:24.460
first of all, what I
get for a vaccine class,

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:25.293
we would have--

00:04:25.293 --> 00:04:26.720
He would be the only lecturer

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:31.010
who would have his own fan base in class,

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:34.960
and there would be a
line for people taking,

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.250
initially all school photos,

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:39.545
but increasingly selfies with him,

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:43.883
and getting his book signed,
copies of his book signed.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:49.480
One of the most remarkable
thing about Bill Foege

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:53.210
is that he's not only
a scientist, but he is,

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:57.440
if I could say so, the original
social justice warriors,

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:02.440
in the sense, focusing on
equity as part of the core DNA

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.160
of public health and global health.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:11.910
Before, we had labels around these issues.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:16.270
And so it is my pleasure
and honor to talk to him

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:18.640
about smallpox eradication,

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.230
but also having a forward
looking discussion

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:25.830
of what are the implications
on future eradication efforts,

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:28.280
future global health and
public health efforts,

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.880
and what are some of the lessons

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:35.760
us mere mortals can learn
from giants like Dr. Foege.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.453
So welcome Dr. Foege.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.233
- Thanks, Saad.

00:05:40.233 --> 00:05:41.410
Thanks for having me.

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:43.300
Thanks for being a professor at Emory.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.070
Thanks for bringing
some glitter to my name.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:49.930
And it's good to be on this program.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:50.763
- My pleasure.

00:05:50.763 --> 00:05:52.663
So the first question is simple,

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:54.993
is the eradication worth it?

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.720
- Let me divide that into two things,

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:02.080
one has to do with people
and the other with money.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.070
For people, think of
the fact that WHO said

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:11.070
in the 20th century, 300
million people died of smallpox.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:14.800
Since most all of them
died in the first 75 years,

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.740
that means there were 4
million deaths a year.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:22.610
And that is about as high as any disease.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:26.250
I mean, tuberculosis and
measles were in that category,

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:28.040
but 4 million deaths a year plus

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.703
4 million people scarred for life.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:36.380
And so from the personal
point of view, it's worth it.

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:40.130
Then you look at the money,
and it's said that it cost

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:44.450
about $300 million to eradicate smallpox,

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:46.210
half of this came from the United States.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:50.360
Well, the United States
has recouped its investment

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:54.530
every three months because
of what it used to cost

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:57.210
to treat people who had complications

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:59.410
because of the vaccine, who died,

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:02.700
or the fact that we
had to have quarantine.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:06.550
So since smallpox has been eradicated,

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:10.800
we have recouped our investment 130 times,

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.420
every dollar that was invested
has come back with $130.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.290
So yes, it's worth it.

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:20.600
In many ways, we borrow from our children.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:23.250
And when you look at climate
change and what we've done

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:25.850
to the plastic in the ocean and so forth,

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:28.410
we're depending on our
children and grandchildren

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:29.830
to pay that off.

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:34.240
Well, disease eradication is
one way that we pay forward,

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:37.540
and we actually give a
gift to our grandchildren.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:38.743
So yes, it's worth it.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:41.370
- Wonderful.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:45.510
So, we know, and then those of us

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:48.960
who have read your writings about it,

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.720
so small blocks eradication
ran into roadblocks

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:57.150
and had areas of efficiency.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:08:01.640
So how many of those
roadblocks or positive aspects

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:04.120
are applicable in the current day?

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:06.370
Are there any new difficulties
or new challenges,

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:11.370
or areas that have improved
since smallpox was eradicated?

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:16.500
- I think every day during the
Smallpox Eradication Program

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.950
was a problem-solving day.

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:21.800
And we looked for people that had a record

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:24.350
of being problem solvers.

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:29.350
So in 2020, we expected to
have a meeting in Atlanta

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:35.090
to celebrate 40 years
since the WHO declaration.

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:37.210
And as part of that, we were looking at,

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:41.540
what are the positive lessons
that we learned from smallpox?

00:08:41.540 --> 00:08:42.940
Then the pandemic came along,

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:44.940
we could not have that meeting.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.270
So instead, we continued to work on,

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:50.260
what are the lessons learned,

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:52.710
and could we put them into a curriculum

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:55.690
which we've done under the umbrella

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:59.160
of becoming better ancestors
that would actually

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.610
have something to say
for today and tomorrow?

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:05.350
So it so happened that we
were looking at these lessons

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:09.050
during the coronavirus pandemic,

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:12.540
and I'm just gonna go
through the nine lessons

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:16.840
we focused on very quickly,
to show you the difference

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:19.080
between smallpox and coronavirus.

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:23.337
Number one lesson is, this
is a cause and effect world.

00:09:23.337 --> 00:09:25.817
And Sphene Hawking says this in his book,

00:09:25.817 --> 00:09:27.640
"A Short History of Time,"

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:29.500
that the whole history of science

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:33.230
is the gradual realization
that things do not happen

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:36.890
in an arbitrary way, it's
a cause and effect world.

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:40.080
And so on coronavirus, we have a president

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:42.180
that says it's gonna disappear like magic.

00:09:43.090 --> 00:09:46.060
Then a second lesson is,
you have to know the truth.

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:49.860
And there were times when we
did not want to know the truth.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.320
And I remember October of 1973,

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.070
our first village to
village search in India,

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:58.000
and in just two states,

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.560
we found 10,000 new cases of smallpox

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:05.057
that no one knew existed
in just six days time.

00:10:05.057 --> 00:10:07.980
And some people said,
"Let's never do this again."

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:09.700
But of course you have to do it again,

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:12.410
it's the best surveillance
you've ever had.

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.240
So there are many times
you don't want the truth,

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.320
but you need the truth
in order to continue.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:22.030
But a third lesson is, you
don't do anything alone,

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:23.920
it requires coalitions.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:27.330
So in this country, we should
have had, for coronavirus,

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:30.047
a coalition that went from
national level that said,

00:10:30.047 --> 00:10:33.360
"Here are some guidelines,
and you can change these

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:36.860
in states and cities and
counties as you need to."

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:40.827
But instead, our government
said to the state,

00:10:40.827 --> 00:10:43.970
"You're all on your own to compete.

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:47.590
Figure out this on your own
and compete for resources."

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:52.590
The next lesson that we
learned was, avoid certainty.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:55.497
And Richard Feynman, the physicist said,

00:10:55.497 --> 00:10:58.280
"Physics is the most certain of sciences,

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:00.030
and it's uncertain."

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:05.030
And he said, "Certainty is the
Achilles' heel of science."

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:07.730
And of course, it is of
politics and religion,

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:09.830
and everything else.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:13.880
But we had so much certainty
given by various people

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:16.980
at press conferences on coronavirus.

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:20.940
The fifth lesson, you have
to continuously evaluate

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:23.130
and change what you're doing.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:26.470
In India, this meant we
had a meeting every month

00:11:26.470 --> 00:11:30.220
in endemic states to find
out what worked last month,

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:31.250
what didn't work,

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:34.440
how do we change our
tactics the next month?

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:37.870
And in this country with coronavirus,

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:40.160
we didn't have that kind of evaluation

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:42.540
and changing of what was happening.

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:45.800
A sixth lesson, respect the culture.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:49.000
And we know that if you
go in with your ideas

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:52.750
of how to do things,
culture will always win.

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:55.360
So if you tangle with
culture, cultural will win,

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:58.080
you have to respect it from the beginning.

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:01.700
The next lesson was, the trade-off between

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:04.640
science and management, the best decisions

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:06.340
come from the best science,

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:09.180
the best results come
from the best management.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:13.120
So how do you put science
and management together?

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:18.050
The eighth lesson, you
need political support.

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:20.960
And this just becomes absolutely essential

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:23.280
that you have politics involved.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:26.997
And many people have
concluded after coronavirus,

00:12:26.997 --> 00:12:30.350
"We don't want politicians
involved in public health."

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:33.220
That's absolutely the wrong conclusion,

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:36.290
that it's the politicians that decide

00:12:36.290 --> 00:12:38.170
on the money that we get.

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:40.730
We need them to help solve the problems,

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:44.220
become part of the solution,
to be invested in the solution,

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:47.100
rather than saying we wanna
separate ourselves from them.

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:50.650
And the last lesson is,
no matter where you live,

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:52.990
you're both global and local.

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:55.930
And so wherever you're
practicing public health,

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:58.830
you're practicing global public health.

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:01.240
And the objective of global public health

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:03.310
is global health equity.

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:05.990
So everyone in public health is working

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:08.590
on global health equity.

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:13.530
Well, no one in public
health even imagined

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:16.187
in the midst of a pandemic
that we would leave WHO.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:19.540
So all of this is to show how the lessons

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:22.890
that we learned from
smallpox were ignored.

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:26.120
Which leads to the 10th and last lesson,

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:28.373
lessons are useless if ignored.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.060
- Now that's very insightful.

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:36.063
And a couple of things
specifically strike me.

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:42.220
You have to engage our elected leaders,

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:44.780
especially in democracies
and even otherwise,

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:47.040
but it doesn't have to be partisan.

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:49.120
So political doesn't have to be partisan.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:52.440
And you've shown, you
and several other leaders

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:56.820
have been well respected
on both sides of the aisle,

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:00.830
and have been able to make
the case for public health,

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:04.323
which is obviously harder
because as you have often said,

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:07.250
public health is effective
and nothing happens.

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:11.140
And so therefore, it is challenging,

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:13.350
but I think it's doable.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:14.780
So switching gears a little bit,

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:19.626
CDC has identified the
virus that causes smallpox

00:14:19.626 --> 00:14:23.203
as a potential bioterrorism threat.

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:29.170
In a changing world
where biological capacity

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:34.040
is increasingly available
beyond a few specialized labs,

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:36.730
and there are perverse incentives

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:41.730
for state and non-state actors to engage

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:45.180
in activities that are
not necessarily ethical,

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:49.263
how do we reduce and mitigate
or even eliminate the risk?

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:54.160
- I think bioterrorism with smallpox virus

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:57.000
has been a concern right
from the beginning.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:02.000
And particularly, before the
United States invaded Iraq,

00:15:02.420 --> 00:15:04.350
and there were questions about,

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:06.900
did Iraq have the virus,

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:10.540
and were they using it for
research on bioterrorism?

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:13.740
And in fact, there was a story,

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:15.320
front page of the New York Times,

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:17.680
saying they did have the virus

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:20.210
that had been provided to them by Russia.

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:23.930
And that it was a virus that
you could not protect against

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:25.693
with our current vaccine.

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:28.580
Well, the administration
came up with a plan

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:31.460
to first vaccinate first responders,

00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:33.480
then they would go to
10 million beyond that.

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:35.440
And then the question was,

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:37.690
would they go to the
whole population or not?

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:43.620
The Institute of Medicine was
asked to oversee that program.

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:45.820
And I was asked to be a member of it,

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:50.180
but I was told that we
could not produce a report

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:52.710
that criticized the administration.

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:54.590
I was taken back by that.

00:15:54.590 --> 00:15:57.300
And I said, "Well, I
can't be part of it then."

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:00.810
Later they came and they asked
if I would be a consultant

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:03.540
if I was not able to vote or anything,

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:06.020
and then I could say anything I wanted.

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:10.020
And so I did do an op-ed
piece for the Washington Post,

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:14.270
and pointed out, if it's
true that they have a virus

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:16.560
that you cannot protect
against with vaccine,

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:19.340
why are we spending so much attention

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:21.690
on vaccination in this country?

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:26.690
And number two, why don't we
realize that if there's a case,

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:30.423
the problem in the United
States will be panic.

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:34.840
It won't be the disease
itself, it'll be the panic.

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:38.340
And the plan was that they
could get to 10 million people

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:40.960
in 10 days of vaccination.

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:42.810
I said, if there were cases,

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.600
we would have 300 million people

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:48.690
trying to get in that line of 10 million,

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:51.640
and it's the panic that
would cause the problem.

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:56.540
I proposed a solution to CDC
and they didn't accept it.

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:59.560
And so I simply put it
as a one page summary

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:04.120
in my book on, "House on
Fire," which is very simple,

00:17:04.120 --> 00:17:06.960
that you look at the 3000 counties

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:11.500
as the point at which we
would respond to smallpox.

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:14.610
We would in each county
use the high schools

00:17:14.610 --> 00:17:16.710
as the point of vaccination.

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:21.020
It's very easy to estimate
how many people live

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:24.610
in the catchment area of a high school.

00:17:24.610 --> 00:17:28.230
If you could tell the
entire country, if need be,

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:30.640
we could vaccinate everyone
in three days time,

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:34.230
and that would prevent the
next cases of smallpox,

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:35.703
you could prevent the panic.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:38.450
You can train people in 10 minutes

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:41.850
to do vaccination with
a bifurcated needle.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:44.630
So we would know how many
vaccinators we would need,

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:46.820
and we would get nurses,
we would get teachers,

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:49.530
we could volunteers and retired people.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:53.190
So you could line all
of this up in advance,

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:55.810
you could keep the vaccine
at a national level

00:17:55.810 --> 00:18:00.710
and send it out overnight to
3000 counties, if you had to.

00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:03.130
And once you can assure the country,

00:18:03.130 --> 00:18:06.430
everyone could be vaccinated
in three days time,

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:08.483
you take away the panic factor.

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:13.730
Now, it's relatively easy
to do that with smallpox,

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:17.850
it's much more complicated
to do this with other agents.

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:21.503
But we weren't even able to come up

00:18:21.503 --> 00:18:23.690
with a reasonable response to smallpox,

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:26.040
so you can see the difficulties

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:27.993
of a response to other problems.

00:18:29.474 --> 00:18:34.474
- No, that's an interesting
point that you highlighted,

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:38.830
that things vary by pathogen,

00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:41.710
but also that there are things

00:18:41.710 --> 00:18:43.150
that you can do even about panic.

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:46.610
And sometimes we are paralyzed

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:50.570
by the potential fear of public reaction,

00:18:50.570 --> 00:18:53.810
where we often forget the basic principles

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:56.390
of public health and public
health communication.

00:18:56.390 --> 00:19:01.390
Well, in every other
School of Public Health,

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:04.160
or in every School of Public Health,

00:19:05.170 --> 00:19:10.070
there is a discussion in every semester

00:19:10.070 --> 00:19:13.483
about the value of vertical
versus horizontal programs.

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:17.220
You led one of the most vertical programs

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:20.220
and then have worked on enhancing

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:24.440
some of the most horizontal
programs, et cetera.

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:28.307
So what are the implications

00:19:28.307 --> 00:19:30.640
of vertical disease specific programs

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:33.730
versus horizontal programs

00:19:33.730 --> 00:19:35.680
and their impact on primary healthcare?

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:39.650
- So you can put vertical
versus horizontal

00:19:39.650 --> 00:19:43.750
on any program and get
people gathering in a room

00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:47.140
because there are strong
feelings on both sides.

00:19:47.140 --> 00:19:51.530
And I came to a conclusion in the 1980s

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:58.000
when I was President of the American

00:19:58.000 --> 00:19:59.720
Public Health Association for a year

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:01.940
and had to give the keynote address.

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:04.070
And so I decided to go back 100 years

00:20:04.070 --> 00:20:08.680
to see what they were discussing
100 years earlier at APHA.

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:12.280
And to my total surprise,
I found that the discussion

00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:14.520
was vertical versus horizontal.

00:20:14.520 --> 00:20:16.410
I thought that that was a fairly

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:18.720
recent global health discussion,

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:20.080
and here it went back 100 years.

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:21.297
So I said to myself,

00:20:21.297 --> 00:20:25.230
"Well, in 100 years, what
happened in this country?"

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:29.823
And it was very clear, every
time we had a tool, we used it,

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:32.440
which is vertical.

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:36.940
And every time we used it, we
improved the infrastructure,

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:38.830
which was horizontal.

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:41.660
And that made it easier
to take the next tool

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:42.770
and include it.

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:46.760
So it was always a ratcheting
up, a vertical, horizontal,

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:48.510
vertical, horizontal.

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:51.650
And so there should be no
discussion of which is best,

00:20:51.650 --> 00:20:53.660
they're both needed.

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:58.320
And so, we have to actually educate people

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:00.070
in how to make that happen

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:02.120
so that this doesn't become the argument.

00:21:03.630 --> 00:21:04.980
- Yeah, no, you're right.

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:08.433
And then sometimes it's
presented as a false choice.

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:12.320
So switching gears a little bit again,

00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:17.110
and what have been some
salient, for lack of word,

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:21.310
failures and successes, and triumphs

00:21:21.310 --> 00:21:22.783
in the COVID 19 pandemic?

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:27.490
- It really was a triumphant tragedy.

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:29.390
The tragedy I've just pointed out,

00:21:29.390 --> 00:21:31.800
was the nine lessons that were ignored.

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:36.720
The triumph is, look at
what happened with vaccines,

00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:40.000
I went back in my journal
and found that the first note

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:44.430
I ever made on messenger
RNA was 25 years ago

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:46.210
after hearing a lecture.

00:21:46.210 --> 00:21:48.210
And I wondered what
would happen after that.

00:21:48.210 --> 00:21:51.690
So you see how long people
have been working on that.

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:53.070
For much of the public,

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:57.390
they think that the work was
just in the last year or two,

00:21:57.390 --> 00:22:00.350
or three, and here it's
been going on all the time.

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:03.660
This was the triumph of coronavirus,

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:06.600
a vaccine that came online so fast,

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:08.830
where you had government support.

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:12.190
And the other part of this triumph is,

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:15.230
we have for the last 30 years,

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:20.230
averaged about one new
disease problem a year.

00:22:20.410 --> 00:22:24.940
Each time we get excited
about it, we get funds,

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:27.130
and we think this is
finally going to change

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:29.790
the infrastructure of public health.

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:32.290
And we are soon going to reach

00:22:32.290 --> 00:22:36.180
the same level as fire stations.

00:22:36.180 --> 00:22:40.300
That is, if a fire station in an airport

00:22:40.300 --> 00:22:42.180
doesn't have a disaster for 10 years,

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:44.223
no one wants to reduce their budget.

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:48.060
Everyone understands you
have to keep funding it.

00:22:48.060 --> 00:22:50.090
So we always think,
that's what's gonna happen

00:22:50.090 --> 00:22:53.340
in public health, we'll get an
infrastructure that is solid

00:22:53.340 --> 00:22:56.200
and continues on, and it never happens.

00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:57.780
I thought with coronavirus,

00:22:57.780 --> 00:22:59.710
for sure this is going to happen.

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:02.470
I no longer believe that
that's going to happen.

00:23:02.470 --> 00:23:05.840
And we have to figure out how
to make that actually happen

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:08.240
so that it's part of the
triumph of coronavirus.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:13.760
- Yeah. So in terms of the premier

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:17.610
Public Health Agency in
this country, the CDC,

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:19.390
what should the CDC look like

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:21.680
after this pandemic is under control?

00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:25.970
Or while obviously, it will take some time

00:23:25.970 --> 00:23:28.810
to bring the pandemic fully
under control, we can start now.

00:23:28.810 --> 00:23:31.613
So what should be the feature of the CDC?

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:37.470
- There are three or four
things Saad, that I think of,

00:23:37.470 --> 00:23:40.420
you notice that the field of public health

00:23:40.420 --> 00:23:42.310
has continued to increase.

00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:45.960
It was pretty much infectious
diseases when I started it,

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:49.230
and became interested
in this 70 years ago,

00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:52.020
but it's then gone from
occupational health

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:54.470
to environmental health,
to chronic diseases,

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:58.670
to violence and injury,
it keeps increasing.

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:02.880
We still have a couple of
areas that should be included,

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:05.080
mental health is one of them.

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:09.010
It's been very hard to make
this a public health issue,

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:10.950
but it has to be, because we have to have

00:24:10.950 --> 00:24:13.840
surveillance systems for mental health,

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:18.700
that we know who requires
treatment and how to proceed.

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:21.320
The other thing is, I think public health

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:24.520
should be taking a lead
on actually looking

00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:29.520
on health outcomes by the
approach with clinical medicine.

00:24:30.450 --> 00:24:34.500
In this country, we are satisfied

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:37.910
to reward people on the basis of process.

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:42.050
So how many tests are
done for this or that,

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:44.600
and how many pap smears and so forth,

00:24:44.600 --> 00:24:47.000
rather than on outcome.

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:50.070
And it always surprises
people to know we spend

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:53.320
twice as much per capita
as any place else,

00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:58.210
and yet we're not in the
top five or 10, or 15, or 20

00:24:58.210 --> 00:25:01.020
countries when it comes
to health outcomes.

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:02.900
So that's one problem.

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:07.210
Number two, I think we
simply have to re-look at,

00:25:07.210 --> 00:25:11.303
what's the science, the
technology, the skills

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:16.800
that are needed at CDC to
keep it at the cutting edge

00:25:16.800 --> 00:25:17.843
of public health.

00:25:18.820 --> 00:25:22.810
They've taken a step by
having a person from HHS,

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:25.190
now take a month to look at CDC

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:27.400
and come up with suggestions.

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:30.730
But we all know that things have become

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:33.910
so political in this country
that half the country

00:25:33.910 --> 00:25:38.910
will not accept what comes
from inside HHS to CDC.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:41.440
And I think we need an outside group

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:44.810
looking at the things I just
mentioned, of technology,

00:25:44.810 --> 00:25:46.030
skills and so forth.

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:48.890
And I would like to see the
National Academy of Medicine

00:25:48.890 --> 00:25:50.440
do such a study.

00:25:50.440 --> 00:25:54.680
Number three, I think that CDC

00:25:54.680 --> 00:25:59.680
should have a series of
conferences with states,

00:26:00.850 --> 00:26:05.850
counties, cities, tribes,
and ask the question,

00:26:08.150 --> 00:26:13.150
how do we get the approach to
public health in this country

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:16.473
as seamless as it was 40 years ago?

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:19.750
Because 40 years ago we had--

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:23.940
CDC had no more authority,
they could not go in to a state

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:28.940
and investigate an (mumbles) cum-problem.

00:26:29.750 --> 00:26:33.480
And they could not decide on
who would do what in the state.

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:37.070
They always had to ask for
an invitation, but it worked.

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:38.010
And why did it work?

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:41.400
Because there was trust,
and trust is the glue

00:26:41.400 --> 00:26:44.020
that keeps a coalition together.

00:26:44.020 --> 00:26:45.420
So we can get back to that,

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:47.780
but it's gonna require
a series of meetings

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:49.890
with all of the people involved.

00:26:49.890 --> 00:26:51.340
It's a different time.

00:26:51.340 --> 00:26:55.160
When I was in the Health
Department in Colorado,

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:59.150
the Health Director in Colorado
had been there 30 years,

00:26:59.150 --> 00:27:00.820
that doesn't happen anymore.

00:27:00.820 --> 00:27:03.890
People are there for two
years and they change

00:27:03.890 --> 00:27:05.820
as soon as the governor changes.

00:27:05.820 --> 00:27:07.710
It is the same thing at CDC.

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:11.143
I was CDC director under
both Carter and Reagan,

00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:15.880
you can't see that happening again.

00:27:15.880 --> 00:27:18.360
But that's the next
thing that CDC has to do,

00:27:18.360 --> 00:27:20.907
is figure out how to make a
seamless approach to this.

00:27:20.907 --> 00:27:25.480
And the last thing, I think
they need to get some outside

00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:28.070
consultation on communications.

00:27:28.070 --> 00:27:33.070
How would it be best for health
workers around the country?

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:34.810
For instance, CDC puts out

00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:39.340
the Morbidity, Mortality
Weekly Report each week,

00:27:39.340 --> 00:27:43.180
they could send this to all
health officers the night before

00:27:43.180 --> 00:27:46.480
with a brief on, what
are the complications

00:27:46.480 --> 00:27:47.720
to expect the next day?

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:51.480
So everybody's looking
at the same material.

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:55.710
So there are things that could
be done to improve and help

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:57.400
CDC at this time.

00:27:57.400 --> 00:27:59.450
It's critical that we have a CDC.

00:28:00.950 --> 00:28:04.920
- Yeah, no, the CDC is
essential for this country

00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:09.050
and globally, because of
its global role as well.

00:28:09.050 --> 00:28:11.340
So you alluded to trust

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:14.180
in the Centers for Disease
Control and Prevention,

00:28:14.180 --> 00:28:16.970
and you alluded to trust
between institutions,

00:28:16.970 --> 00:28:18.590
between the CDC and the states, et cetera.

00:28:18.590 --> 00:28:22.230
But there's a broader, unfortunate problem

00:28:22.230 --> 00:28:24.910
of public trust in CDC,
and it has been measured,

00:28:24.910 --> 00:28:26.580
and it has declined.

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:29.850
There was another period
where the trust in CDC

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:33.300
was dented a bit, which was around

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:36.753
the Swine Flu Vaccine Program in 1976,

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:43.970
where there was this alarm
for a potential pandemic

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:47.573
or a large outbreak, there was
a vaccine program rolled out,

00:28:47.573 --> 00:28:50.710
there were some adverse events associated

00:28:50.710 --> 00:28:52.390
with the vaccine program,

00:28:52.390 --> 00:28:56.320
with a vaccine related
to G-barre Syndrome,

00:28:56.320 --> 00:29:00.310
which turned out to be
true but not at a level

00:29:00.310 --> 00:29:03.710
where perhaps it changed
the risk, benefit risk.

00:29:03.710 --> 00:29:06.980
But there was some
lessons for communications

00:29:06.980 --> 00:29:08.300
and policy, et cetera.

00:29:08.300 --> 00:29:10.940
And I'm describing it,
not for you, obviously,

00:29:10.940 --> 00:29:12.300
you know this by heart,

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:16.010
I'm just setting the stage for those of us

00:29:16.010 --> 00:29:19.480
who were doing other things at that time,

00:29:19.480 --> 00:29:21.470
like being in kindergarten.
(William laughing)

00:29:21.470 --> 00:29:26.470
So I'm not that young,
but our audience is.

00:29:26.750 --> 00:29:31.750
But going back to that
period in that context,

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:36.973
but also beyond that,

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:42.087
any lessons on restoring
public trust in the CDC?

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:47.740
- Well, you're right to point out my age,

00:29:47.740 --> 00:29:51.130
because I was actually involved in that.

00:29:51.130 --> 00:29:52.930
And I attended the--

00:29:52.930 --> 00:29:55.090
Dave Sencer was the director of CDC,

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:59.080
and I attended the very
first PD he ever had on this,

00:29:59.080 --> 00:30:03.460
with the Advisory Committee
on Immunization Practices.

00:30:03.460 --> 00:30:05.973
It was an emergency meeting on a Saturday.

00:30:06.930 --> 00:30:11.300
And what was presented was,
three things have happened,

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:12.563
we have a new virus.

00:30:13.560 --> 00:30:17.360
Number two, we have evidence
of human-to-human spread

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:19.700
Fort Dix, New Jersey.

00:30:19.700 --> 00:30:24.030
And number three, there is no
immunity in the population.

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:26.930
Those three things had
never happened before

00:30:26.930 --> 00:30:29.283
without resulting in a pandemic.

00:30:30.250 --> 00:30:34.117
And so it wasn't difficult
for the group to say,

00:30:34.117 --> 00:30:36.380
"There's gonna be a pandemic."

00:30:36.380 --> 00:30:38.543
The discussion though, revolved around,

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:40.650
we have to make vaccine,

00:30:40.650 --> 00:30:43.960
do we store the vaccine in refrigerators,

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:45.683
or do we store it in people?

00:30:46.550 --> 00:30:49.290
Well, if you store it in refrigerators,

00:30:49.290 --> 00:30:52.100
we know from experience,
that a flu outbreak

00:30:52.100 --> 00:30:54.913
can go across this
country in two weeks time.

00:30:55.970 --> 00:30:58.310
If you vaccinate someone with a vaccine,

00:30:58.310 --> 00:31:01.580
it may take two weeks
for them to get immunity.

00:31:01.580 --> 00:31:04.650
So there's really no
way that you can justify

00:31:04.650 --> 00:31:07.990
waiting until you have spread of the virus

00:31:07.990 --> 00:31:09.620
before you start vaccinating.

00:31:09.620 --> 00:31:13.110
That's why there was a
decision on vaccinating.

00:31:13.110 --> 00:31:16.870
It was not a decision made in isolation.

00:31:16.870 --> 00:31:21.340
Sencer did not even take a
vote of the committee that day.

00:31:21.340 --> 00:31:23.600
And I asked him
afterwards, why didn't you?

00:31:23.600 --> 00:31:26.930
And he said, "Because this is
gonna be a political problem."

00:31:26.930 --> 00:31:28.770
And he said, "I need to absorb it

00:31:28.770 --> 00:31:31.580
rather than have the committee absorb it."

00:31:31.580 --> 00:31:34.143
But it went all the
way to the Oval Office.

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:38.023
And in the Oval Office
with President Ford,

00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:42.510
with Sencer presenting what we knew,

00:31:42.510 --> 00:31:45.750
they had in the group, Salk, and Sabin,

00:31:45.750 --> 00:31:48.570
and some of the best
virologists of the day,

00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:52.277
and they all told Ford,
"This was the thing to do."

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:56.430
Then as soon as we ran
into the first cases

00:31:56.430 --> 00:32:01.430
of Guillain-Barre, suddenly
people started jumping ship.

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:05.020
And eventually, it was only Dave Sencer

00:32:05.870 --> 00:32:08.800
who was being blamed
for what had happened.

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:11.733
The program was stopped
because of Guillain-Barre,

00:32:12.640 --> 00:32:17.640
and Sencer was fired by Joseph Califano,

00:32:17.680 --> 00:32:21.530
actually not by Califano,
but by his deputy.

00:32:21.530 --> 00:32:26.530
And so that's how difficult
the political situation

00:32:26.600 --> 00:32:28.220
was at that time.

00:32:28.220 --> 00:32:32.520
But CDC did not lose its credibility.

00:32:32.520 --> 00:32:36.260
And therefore, even though
there was some reduction

00:32:36.260 --> 00:32:40.720
in the glue of trust,
it all came back again.

00:32:40.720 --> 00:32:43.907
And particularly with
Legionnaire's disease

00:32:43.907 --> 00:32:48.290
and the solving of that
problem, the trust came back.

00:32:48.290 --> 00:32:53.290
At the present time, the trust
factor is really important,

00:32:54.460 --> 00:32:59.460
because I see public health
people not trusting each other,

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:04.430
I see the public not
trusting public health.

00:33:04.430 --> 00:33:09.430
And 30 years ago, I gave a talk at CDC,

00:33:10.210 --> 00:33:13.360
and I was talking about
how there are people who,

00:33:13.360 --> 00:33:17.600
like Gandhi, did the right things,

00:33:17.600 --> 00:33:20.510
but we don't know about them in history.

00:33:20.510 --> 00:33:22.640
But I said, "There are also institutions

00:33:22.640 --> 00:33:25.490
that become Gandhian institutions,

00:33:25.490 --> 00:33:27.337
and that CDC was one of them."

00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:32.250
But that reputation doesn't
go on into the future,

00:33:32.250 --> 00:33:35.190
it has to be earned every day.

00:33:35.190 --> 00:33:38.110
And that's the challenge of CDC right now,

00:33:38.110 --> 00:33:40.760
is to earn that reputation back every day.

00:33:40.760 --> 00:33:42.253
And I think it can be done.

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:47.410
- Interesting. So now,
I'm taking some questions

00:33:47.410 --> 00:33:51.710
from the audience and those
of you who are listening in,

00:33:51.710 --> 00:33:54.040
please feel free to add your questions.

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:55.710
So I already have a few questions.

00:33:55.710 --> 00:33:57.370
And the first one is,

00:33:57.370 --> 00:33:59.890
will you please compare
the eradication of smallpox

00:33:59.890 --> 00:34:04.881
to the effort to eradicate
the Guinea worm disease,

00:34:04.881 --> 00:34:08.830
which has been described, et cetera,

00:34:08.830 --> 00:34:13.830
including in the book by Don
Hopkins and Ernesto Ruiz-Tiben.

00:34:15.180 --> 00:34:18.730
I know that you have been
engaged with both individuals

00:34:18.730 --> 00:34:21.000
and institutions like the Carter Center,

00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:23.970
who have been at the
forefront of the program.

00:34:23.970 --> 00:34:28.970
So any lessons that translated,

00:34:30.470 --> 00:34:34.210
and any lessons that did
not translate or transfer

00:34:34.210 --> 00:34:36.270
between the Smallpox Program

00:34:36.270 --> 00:34:41.173
and the Guinea Worm Disease Program?

00:34:42.470 --> 00:34:46.290
- First, I think all nine
lessons do in fact transfer.

00:34:46.290 --> 00:34:50.730
But Guinea worm had some
problems and barriers

00:34:50.730 --> 00:34:51.710
that smallpox didn't.

00:34:51.710 --> 00:34:56.440
With smallpox, it was very
hard to keep cases hidden,

00:34:56.440 --> 00:35:00.030
because the disease was
on the face and the hands.

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:04.310
And so people deliberately
would hide cases,

00:35:04.310 --> 00:35:06.180
but it was hard, difficult.

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:09.540
With Guinea worm, the
disease wasn't even known

00:35:09.540 --> 00:35:12.220
in the capital cities,
it wasn't even known

00:35:12.220 --> 00:35:14.370
where you had the Health Department.

00:35:14.370 --> 00:35:18.060
And I think Nigeria turns out
to be a good example of that,

00:35:18.060 --> 00:35:20.900
that they were reporting
5,000 or 6,000 cases

00:35:20.900 --> 00:35:22.250
of Guinea worm a year.

00:35:22.250 --> 00:35:25.090
Well, that didn't seem
like a very big problem.

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:30.090
But there was a one-time
survey done in Nigeria

00:35:30.280 --> 00:35:33.020
that showed, at one point in time,

00:35:33.020 --> 00:35:38.020
almost 700,000 cases of Guinea worm.

00:35:38.230 --> 00:35:43.020
And for those 700,000 people,
it was hard to go to school,

00:35:43.020 --> 00:35:45.020
it was hard to work in the field.

00:35:45.020 --> 00:35:49.100
I mean, this really did
cause a change in lifestyle,

00:35:49.100 --> 00:35:52.340
but Ministry of Health
didn't know about this.

00:35:52.340 --> 00:35:57.340
But with that survey in Nigeria,
the president of Nigeria,

00:35:57.800 --> 00:36:02.240
President Yakubu Gowon was so
surprised by that result.

00:36:02.240 --> 00:36:06.400
He put up a million dollars
in Nigerian money that day

00:36:06.400 --> 00:36:08.440
for Guinea worm eradication.

00:36:08.440 --> 00:36:12.430
So it shows the need
for political support,

00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:15.700
and how do you get that
kind of political support.

00:36:15.700 --> 00:36:17.930
They've also run into a problem of course,

00:36:17.930 --> 00:36:21.250
with animals and particularly dogs

00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:23.343
that transmit Guinea worm.

00:36:24.220 --> 00:36:25.770
We knew from the beginning

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:28.640
that there was Guinea worm
reported in some animals,

00:36:28.640 --> 00:36:29.760
but it was always--

00:36:29.760 --> 00:36:31.360
It looked like a dead end,

00:36:31.360 --> 00:36:34.200
not something that went to humans.

00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:37.840
And now in Chad, there's
some certain places

00:36:37.840 --> 00:36:41.320
where a lot of dogs have Guinea worm.

00:36:41.320 --> 00:36:43.900
I think that this problem
is going to be solved,

00:36:43.900 --> 00:36:47.190
there were only 15 cases of
human Guinea worm last year

00:36:47.190 --> 00:36:49.660
in the entire world, 15,

00:36:49.660 --> 00:36:52.750
compare that to the millions
that we used to have.

00:36:52.750 --> 00:36:57.750
But here's the problem, that
we have to evolve faster

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:00.000
than the organism does.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.470
In smallpox, that was always clear to me,

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:07.380
that the approach from the
smallpox virus point of view,

00:37:07.380 --> 00:37:09.270
and work for centuries.

00:37:09.270 --> 00:37:12.330
And now we were evolving
faster than the virus,

00:37:12.330 --> 00:37:13.890
and the virus couldn't keep up.

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:16.190
We have to do the same
thing with Guinea worm.

00:37:17.780 --> 00:37:21.620
- Yeah. So you alluded to
animal reservoirs, et cetera.

00:37:21.620 --> 00:37:25.080
And then when you do
these tabletop exercises

00:37:25.080 --> 00:37:30.080
and diseases are evaluated
potential for eradication,

00:37:30.867 --> 00:37:33.680
and a big no, no, not an absolute no, no,

00:37:33.680 --> 00:37:36.937
but a big no, no, is a
existence of a human--

00:37:38.609 --> 00:37:41.443
A non-human, sorry, reservoir.

00:37:42.840 --> 00:37:46.280
But look, we have been making
investments in one health,

00:37:46.280 --> 00:37:51.280
we are better at measuring infection

00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:56.680
and pathogens in animals,
as well as humans.

00:37:56.680 --> 00:38:00.380
Do you think it's time to be
a little bit more audacious?

00:38:00.380 --> 00:38:01.700
Is there room for audacity,

00:38:01.700 --> 00:38:04.910
to say that this criteria is much softer,

00:38:04.910 --> 00:38:08.283
or should be softer than
has been traditionally?

00:38:09.440 --> 00:38:12.940
And can we be bold enough
to look at diseases

00:38:12.940 --> 00:38:15.160
that have animal reservoirs

00:38:15.160 --> 00:38:17.210
for potential elimination or eradication?

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:23.270
- Absolutely. when you look
at the last 30 diseases

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:28.510
that have come unexpectedly
to our attention,

00:38:28.510 --> 00:38:32.930
75% of them involve an animal or a vector,

00:38:32.930 --> 00:38:37.930
and yet we do not do animal
and human surveillance together

00:38:38.670 --> 00:38:43.320
at CDC or WHO until
there's a specific problem,

00:38:43.320 --> 00:38:46.850
and then we do ad hoc animal surveillance.

00:38:46.850 --> 00:38:49.690
So we really have to figure out how to get

00:38:49.690 --> 00:38:52.473
the one world approach on surveillance.

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:55.350
I haven't looked at this for some years,

00:38:55.350 --> 00:38:57.460
but I think there's only
one place in the world

00:38:57.460 --> 00:39:00.150
that actually has what we used to call,

00:39:00.150 --> 00:39:03.030
a "Class 4 lab for animals,"

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:06.460
and a "Class 4 lab for
humans" in the same city.

00:39:06.460 --> 00:39:08.950
And that's Winnipeg, Canada.

00:39:08.950 --> 00:39:12.010
And it was by accident, two
people that were interested,

00:39:12.010 --> 00:39:14.430
it wasn't part of a big plan.

00:39:14.430 --> 00:39:17.730
So we haven't approached this as a unit

00:39:17.730 --> 00:39:19.883
in the past and we have to from now on.

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:26.230
- Yeah. So a couple of questions from--

00:39:27.230 --> 00:39:28.840
Additional questions,
a few other questions

00:39:28.840 --> 00:39:29.940
from the audience.

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:34.940
And one I think, is
from a former CDC person

00:39:35.560 --> 00:39:37.433
if I recognize the name correctly.

00:39:38.520 --> 00:39:41.580
And they ask, when will the CDC have

00:39:41.580 --> 00:39:45.200
a long term Emergency Response Division,

00:39:45.200 --> 00:39:46.700
a fixed team of professionals

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:49.270
who are assigned to ERD full-time?

00:39:49.270 --> 00:39:50.870
Obviously there is some capacity

00:39:50.870 --> 00:39:54.290
in some organizational
structures that are activated,

00:39:54.290 --> 00:39:56.800
that are semi-permanent, but
I think they're talking about

00:39:56.800 --> 00:39:59.120
something more permanent, more focused,

00:39:59.120 --> 00:40:01.533
and with dedicated resources.

00:40:03.080 --> 00:40:04.490
- Interesting thing here Saad,

00:40:04.490 --> 00:40:08.880
is we had that in the early 1980s,

00:40:08.880 --> 00:40:10.530
and people don't know that.

00:40:10.530 --> 00:40:13.850
But we had a program at CDC

00:40:13.850 --> 00:40:17.800
where we looked at each
organism and asked the question,

00:40:17.800 --> 00:40:22.100
what would we do if we were
trying to use this offensively?

00:40:22.100 --> 00:40:23.930
And therefore, what do we need to do

00:40:23.930 --> 00:40:25.830
to come up with a defense?

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:30.830
This was with CDC and the
FBI, with secure phones,

00:40:33.074 --> 00:40:38.074
secure rooms, and 24
hour a day people on call

00:40:40.290 --> 00:40:44.793
in order to be able to look
at a particular problem.

00:40:45.810 --> 00:40:50.400
I was so pleased what
was happening with this?

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:55.400
When I left, this was
presented to the new director,

00:40:56.770 --> 00:40:59.540
and his response was, "Oh,
this will never happen."

00:40:59.540 --> 00:41:02.400
And he closed on the program overnight.

00:41:02.400 --> 00:41:06.130
So this was in CDCs history,

00:41:06.130 --> 00:41:09.793
and it has to now be in CDCs future.

00:41:11.398 --> 00:41:14.240
- So the next question,
I'm actually combining

00:41:14.240 --> 00:41:15.813
a couple of questions there.

00:41:16.957 --> 00:41:21.957
And one of the question is,
one of our living legends,

00:41:22.100 --> 00:41:26.660
Jerry Friedland here at TL, who did some-

00:41:26.660 --> 00:41:29.220
Has been doing, and has
did some pioneering work

00:41:30.090 --> 00:41:34.023
in the early parts and subsequent
parts of the HIV epidemic.

00:41:35.235 --> 00:41:36.910
I'm combining a couple of questions there.

00:41:36.910 --> 00:41:40.780
So could you please
comment on the ongoing,

00:41:40.780 --> 00:41:44.870
concurrent global pandemics of TB and HIV

00:41:44.870 --> 00:41:48.680
in the context of the
rapid COVID 19 pandemic,

00:41:48.680 --> 00:41:52.010
and what lessons can be
learned, and especially,

00:41:52.010 --> 00:41:54.610
and the related part is that in terms

00:41:54.610 --> 00:41:58.710
of the role of the CDC,
you had just left the CDC

00:41:58.710 --> 00:42:03.710
when HIV came to fore,
in terms of an epidemic,

00:42:06.320 --> 00:42:08.420
and then eventually became a pandemic.

00:42:08.420 --> 00:42:12.450
So any thoughts on HIV
AIDS from the early days,

00:42:12.450 --> 00:42:13.600
and the response to that,

00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:15.800
and then what are the implications

00:42:15.800 --> 00:42:19.530
of COVID 19 and the current situation

00:42:19.530 --> 00:42:23.433
on these global pandemics
of TB and HIV AIDS?

00:42:24.540 --> 00:42:25.580
- Well, as you know Saad,

00:42:25.580 --> 00:42:27.860
I'm not smart enough to
answer these questions.

00:42:27.860 --> 00:42:31.490
But I will give you a
top of the head feeling.

00:42:31.490 --> 00:42:36.270
With HIV, it took quite
some time, didn't it,

00:42:36.270 --> 00:42:41.270
before people saw this
as a global problem.

00:42:41.370 --> 00:42:46.323
I remember those days in
Africa with medical people,

00:42:47.240 --> 00:42:51.360
teachers, missionaries were dying faster

00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:53.080
than they could be replaced,

00:42:53.080 --> 00:42:56.763
and you just had no idea
where this was going to go.

00:42:57.600 --> 00:43:00.610
And it had to be seen as a global problem

00:43:00.610 --> 00:43:03.773
before we could get global solutions.

00:43:05.210 --> 00:43:09.280
Will Durant used to say
that we will never do things

00:43:09.280 --> 00:43:12.903
on a global level unless
we fear an alien invasion.

00:43:13.900 --> 00:43:18.610
And it struck me one day
that we have surrogates

00:43:18.610 --> 00:43:23.080
for an alien invasion,
and HIV eventually became

00:43:23.080 --> 00:43:26.560
one of those surrogates
for alien invasion.

00:43:26.560 --> 00:43:29.390
And so I think, one of the great things

00:43:29.390 --> 00:43:31.670
that George W. Bush did,

00:43:31.670 --> 00:43:34.950
was to put huge amounts of money

00:43:34.950 --> 00:43:38.900
into a global approach to HIV,

00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:42.570
and we've gotta credit
him for having done that,

00:43:42.570 --> 00:43:46.880
But we should be very
careful in public health

00:43:46.880 --> 00:43:51.880
to do other things as surrogates
for an alien invasion.

00:43:51.930 --> 00:43:55.150
It's what probably led
to smallpox eradication,

00:43:55.150 --> 00:43:58.980
because everyone knew
smallpox was a threat,

00:43:58.980 --> 00:44:00.950
even if they hadn't seen it for a while.

00:44:00.950 --> 00:44:04.830
In this country, we had
not had a case since 1949,

00:44:04.830 --> 00:44:09.270
and yet the US participated
because they saw this

00:44:09.270 --> 00:44:13.520
as a threat to the US, not
just to other countries.

00:44:13.520 --> 00:44:17.050
And so that's part of what we
need to do in public health,

00:44:17.050 --> 00:44:21.140
is to show how things
are a threat globally,

00:44:21.140 --> 00:44:24.920
and how they become a surrogate
for an alien invasion.

00:44:24.920 --> 00:44:29.240
Coronavirus, it hurts a lot

00:44:29.240 --> 00:44:33.270
to see how we did not respond globally

00:44:33.270 --> 00:44:34.543
the way we should have.

00:44:35.840 --> 00:44:40.840
The Gavi actually had an
approach to the global program

00:44:41.710 --> 00:44:43.580
where they had a lot of buy-in,

00:44:43.580 --> 00:44:46.250
but they couldn't get
the vaccine at first.

00:44:46.250 --> 00:44:51.110
And much of the enthusiasm
then started disappearing.

00:44:51.110 --> 00:44:55.260
But we have to again,
say every place we are

00:44:55.260 --> 00:44:57.070
is both local and global,

00:44:57.070 --> 00:45:00.503
and that our job is global health equity.

00:45:01.450 --> 00:45:02.930
- Yeah. And so coming to another question.

00:45:02.930 --> 00:45:05.330
So why are the public
health systems in the US

00:45:05.330 --> 00:45:06.203
so fragmented?

00:45:07.690 --> 00:45:09.920
There are obviously states,
county and federal levels,

00:45:09.920 --> 00:45:11.870
but there are different
policies in different states,

00:45:11.870 --> 00:45:14.220
obviously part of it is a feature

00:45:14.220 --> 00:45:16.420
of the system of
governance in this country.

00:45:17.910 --> 00:45:21.480
Because constitutionally, the
police powers of the states

00:45:21.480 --> 00:45:25.050
which include quarantine powers
and all sorts of other stuff

00:45:25.050 --> 00:45:26.610
resides within the states,

00:45:26.610 --> 00:45:27.990
not at the federal government level,

00:45:27.990 --> 00:45:31.790
although there's some
limited exercise of that,

00:45:31.790 --> 00:45:36.300
but we could align them better.

00:45:36.300 --> 00:45:38.880
We managed with the same constitutional

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:42.670
and legal infrastructure, we
managed to have, for example,

00:45:42.670 --> 00:45:46.070
a working Intrastate Highway System,

00:45:46.070 --> 00:45:47.753
public health remains fragmented.

00:45:48.920 --> 00:45:51.313
And so any thoughts on that?

00:45:53.794 --> 00:45:55.610
- And as I mentioned before,

00:45:55.610 --> 00:46:00.610
we did not need more authority
because we had so many people

00:46:00.970 --> 00:46:05.890
from CDC who were actually
working in states.

00:46:05.890 --> 00:46:08.120
We had so many state people who had taken

00:46:08.120 --> 00:46:10.500
part of their training at CDC,

00:46:10.500 --> 00:46:13.930
that this trust factor was
different at that time.

00:46:13.930 --> 00:46:16.920
It's become politicized since then,

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:19.130
because every state governor

00:46:19.130 --> 00:46:23.420
has their own health advisor
now, and they switch a lot.

00:46:23.420 --> 00:46:26.720
But I think we can get back
to that by figuring out,

00:46:26.720 --> 00:46:28.950
what is required to make this seamless?

00:46:28.950 --> 00:46:32.830
What do states need from
the federal government

00:46:32.830 --> 00:46:34.450
to make this happen?

00:46:34.450 --> 00:46:36.950
And so I haven't given up on the idea

00:46:36.950 --> 00:46:40.573
that we don't need more
authority. We need more trust.

00:46:41.490 --> 00:46:43.610
- And perhaps more money. And if I can--

00:46:43.610 --> 00:46:46.743
One thing I have heard
you say a few times,

00:46:48.010 --> 00:46:50.620
heard you quote Dolly Parton a few times,

00:46:50.620 --> 00:46:52.630
that it takes money to look this cheap,

00:46:52.630 --> 00:46:54.610
if I'm paraphrasing that correctly.

00:46:54.610 --> 00:46:56.040
- She said, "You'd be surprised

00:46:56.040 --> 00:46:57.500
how much it costs to look this cheap."

00:46:57.500 --> 00:46:59.903
- Yeah, you said it right.

00:47:01.191 --> 00:47:05.123
And we don't invest enough,

00:47:06.360 --> 00:47:11.360
there are high returns on
investment on public health,

00:47:11.590 --> 00:47:14.880
and we still don't take as
a country, and as a world,

00:47:14.880 --> 00:47:18.210
don't take the rational
decision of investing

00:47:18.210 --> 00:47:19.630
in public health.

00:47:19.630 --> 00:47:20.880
So in terms of--

00:47:20.880 --> 00:47:23.150
We talked about bioterrorism et cetera,

00:47:23.150 --> 00:47:25.710
and there's a direct
question from the audience,

00:47:25.710 --> 00:47:27.630
which says, should the smallpox variants

00:47:27.630 --> 00:47:30.869
in the two labs in Atlanta
and Moscow be destroyed.

00:47:30.869 --> 00:47:35.869
And I would say that that's
a naive assumption if I may,

00:47:36.200 --> 00:47:40.210
that it is only in two
labs, there are undeclared,

00:47:40.210 --> 00:47:43.750
but confirmed reserves, correct me

00:47:43.750 --> 00:47:45.620
if my interpretation is incorrect,

00:47:45.620 --> 00:47:49.623
but still it's limited places
where the virus exists.

00:47:54.400 --> 00:47:56.600
Do you think it's too big a threat

00:47:56.600 --> 00:48:01.600
to have the virus even
present in those two places,

00:48:01.800 --> 00:48:03.000
or other places as well?

00:48:04.010 --> 00:48:08.090
- So one has to look
at, what is the threat

00:48:08.090 --> 00:48:12.160
if we would unilaterally
destroy smallpox virus

00:48:12.160 --> 00:48:13.223
in this country?

00:48:14.070 --> 00:48:18.250
And the vaccine is not made from a virus,

00:48:18.250 --> 00:48:21.370
so we don't need the virus
in order to make vaccine,

00:48:21.370 --> 00:48:25.023
a vaccine is a different virus.

00:48:26.580 --> 00:48:30.030
It's hard to see any
advantage to keeping the virus

00:48:30.030 --> 00:48:35.030
because we now have the genetic code

00:48:35.890 --> 00:48:38.053
of every strain of smallpox virus.

00:48:39.040 --> 00:48:42.900
And we know that it's probably
possible to recreate this

00:48:42.900 --> 00:48:44.750
if someone wanted to,

00:48:44.750 --> 00:48:49.700
I see no advantage for keeping
the virus in either place.

00:48:49.700 --> 00:48:54.400
And I see no advantage to
keeping it in this country

00:48:54.400 --> 00:48:57.500
if Russia refuses to destroy theirs.

00:48:57.500 --> 00:49:01.080
So I would say, we
should destroy our virus

00:49:01.080 --> 00:49:03.433
because I can't see any
advantage to having it.

00:49:04.870 --> 00:49:07.488
- Okay. So there's another question.

00:49:07.488 --> 00:49:10.238
And I'll take the liberty of
modifying it a little bit.

00:49:11.420 --> 00:49:12.920
How do you see the role of WHO

00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:18.400
in conjunction with CDC,
but also as an entity

00:49:18.400 --> 00:49:21.133
that needs to evolve with
the public health threats?

00:49:22.120 --> 00:49:24.903
How do you see the role
of WHO going forward?

00:49:27.170 --> 00:49:30.490
- I always tell people, WHO
is absolutely essential,

00:49:30.490 --> 00:49:33.190
if we didn't have it,
we'd have to create it.

00:49:33.190 --> 00:49:37.590
And so the real question
is, how do we improve it?

00:49:37.590 --> 00:49:41.670
And unfortunately, the United States

00:49:41.670 --> 00:49:46.280
was involved in making
it as weak as it is,

00:49:46.280 --> 00:49:48.510
because when it was created,

00:49:48.510 --> 00:49:51.540
we insisted on strong regional offices

00:49:51.540 --> 00:49:53.743
because we were trying to protect PAHO.

00:49:54.650 --> 00:49:57.310
And so the regional
offices are strong enough,

00:49:57.310 --> 00:50:00.650
they can undermine Geneva
any time they want to.

00:50:00.650 --> 00:50:03.060
And apparently they often want to.

00:50:03.060 --> 00:50:08.060
And so with Ebola in
2016, the first big fight

00:50:08.980 --> 00:50:13.980
was between WHO Geneva and
WHO Afro on who's in charge.

00:50:14.680 --> 00:50:18.392
The one thing they agreed
on, "Don't call in CDC."

00:50:18.392 --> 00:50:21.100
(Saad laughs)
And so this is a problem

00:50:21.100 --> 00:50:25.690
because we made WHO weaker
than it needed to be.

00:50:25.690 --> 00:50:26.840
Then what do we do?

00:50:26.840 --> 00:50:29.410
We give them a Board of Directors

00:50:29.410 --> 00:50:32.670
of 193 ministers of health.

00:50:32.670 --> 00:50:36.510
And there's no one that would
accept a job at a company

00:50:36.510 --> 00:50:40.330
in the United States with a
Board of Directors of 193,

00:50:40.330 --> 00:50:42.270
195 people.

00:50:42.270 --> 00:50:45.480
And third, we join the course every year,

00:50:45.480 --> 00:50:48.270
telling them to reduce their budget.

00:50:48.270 --> 00:50:51.610
And that's where the Dolly
Parton quote comes in,

00:50:51.610 --> 00:50:55.060
that, I said, we will finally learn

00:50:55.060 --> 00:50:57.650
how much it costs to look this cheap.

00:50:57.650 --> 00:51:02.650
And I think 2014 in Africa
with Ebola, we got our answer.

00:51:03.370 --> 00:51:08.370
And we need to support WHO
so it has the same resources

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:13.540
and abilities, and technology
that the world needs to have,

00:51:13.540 --> 00:51:17.810
a strong public health institution.

00:51:17.810 --> 00:51:18.643
- Absolutely. Right.

00:51:18.643 --> 00:51:22.130
And I think that as we
will look back on 2020,

00:51:22.130 --> 00:51:24.650
we will finally account
for the consequences

00:51:24.650 --> 00:51:28.520
of the US pulling out, and
being antagonistic toward WHO.

00:51:28.520 --> 00:51:31.413
So you refer to COVAX,
or you alluded to COVAX,

00:51:31.413 --> 00:51:34.800
that the system that was
created to provide vaccines,

00:51:34.800 --> 00:51:36.780
there were no takers.

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:39.160
Initially they didn't have the money,

00:51:39.160 --> 00:51:41.004
and then they didn't have the backing

00:51:41.004 --> 00:51:43.220
from big countries like the US

00:51:43.220 --> 00:51:48.220
who were working against the
interest of COVAX and WHO.

00:51:48.977 --> 00:51:53.977
And so that gap created the
gap in interest and funding,

00:51:54.870 --> 00:51:57.690
and effort, created such vulnerabilities.

00:51:57.690 --> 00:52:00.510
And I would submit that we
are still paying the price

00:52:00.510 --> 00:52:03.053
in terms of increased risk
of variance, et cetera.

00:52:04.510 --> 00:52:08.720
So one of my colleague is asking,

00:52:08.720 --> 00:52:11.380
that there are a lot of
stories of smallpox eradication

00:52:11.380 --> 00:52:14.430
that includes the stories
from specific countries.

00:52:14.430 --> 00:52:17.660
Somalia, for example, comes up a few times

00:52:19.430 --> 00:52:22.383
as one of the last places with smallpox,

00:52:23.480 --> 00:52:28.040
and India was part of the story of polio,

00:52:28.040 --> 00:52:31.870
and Pakistan and Afghanistan,
and Nigeria, et cetera.

00:52:31.870 --> 00:52:34.560
So what are the implications
of global health governance

00:52:34.560 --> 00:52:36.900
of outbreaks and relationships
between countries

00:52:36.900 --> 00:52:38.530
and international bodies?

00:52:38.530 --> 00:52:41.597
Because yes, even if
you set aside, WHO Afro,

00:52:44.670 --> 00:52:47.560
there was a delicate
dance with the countries,

00:52:47.560 --> 00:52:50.940
and you had firsthand experience
dealing with countries

00:52:50.940 --> 00:52:54.060
both from within, based
on your deployments,

00:52:54.060 --> 00:52:56.883
but in your WHO and CDC roles as well.

00:52:58.130 --> 00:53:01.400
- Well, the interesting
thing was that we were able

00:53:01.400 --> 00:53:06.070
to do this with the Soviet
Union during the Cold War.

00:53:06.070 --> 00:53:11.070
And so in India, I actually
supervised some people

00:53:11.190 --> 00:53:15.730
from the Soviet Union who in
turn supervised Americans.

00:53:15.730 --> 00:53:17.920
Now, you can't imagine that happening

00:53:17.920 --> 00:53:22.080
in any other structure at
that time during the Cold War.

00:53:22.080 --> 00:53:25.430
But with health, we can have objectives

00:53:25.430 --> 00:53:29.840
that are much more important
than the political differences.

00:53:29.840 --> 00:53:33.460
And so that's what WHO
has to keep emphasizing,

00:53:33.460 --> 00:53:35.360
what we have to keep emphasizing,

00:53:35.360 --> 00:53:37.130
that we're willing to work with anybody

00:53:37.130 --> 00:53:38.693
in order to improve health.

00:53:40.400 --> 00:53:43.900
- Yeah. And so a as we wind down,

00:53:43.900 --> 00:53:48.163
and we had sort of last
five minutes of our time,

00:53:50.498 --> 00:53:52.780
I would love for you to talk about

00:53:56.240 --> 00:54:01.240
what advice would you share
with early career investigators,

00:54:03.530 --> 00:54:06.660
young professionals, aspiring
public health professionals,

00:54:06.660 --> 00:54:10.070
et cetera, because a lot of
them are part of our audience

00:54:10.070 --> 00:54:12.933
as students and early
stage faculty, and others.

00:54:14.140 --> 00:54:18.913
And I will share a personal anecdote,

00:54:19.940 --> 00:54:23.550
when I became the Bill Foe
professor in my previous job,

00:54:23.550 --> 00:54:28.280
it was almost funny as if
someone had given a form letter

00:54:28.280 --> 00:54:30.250
to people who wrote to me to congratulate,

00:54:30.250 --> 00:54:33.140
and there was one sentence
that was very consistent,

00:54:33.140 --> 00:54:34.250
surprisingly consistent,

00:54:34.250 --> 00:54:37.850
and it was Bill Foege is
my public health hero.

00:54:37.850 --> 00:54:41.860
And after the first two, three emails,

00:54:41.860 --> 00:54:45.830
and these were people
I respected, et cetera.

00:54:45.830 --> 00:54:48.670
But so from that perspective,

00:54:48.670 --> 00:54:51.660
of someone who has made
contributions to public health,

00:54:51.660 --> 00:54:54.270
who has been witnessed
to a lot of successes,

00:54:54.270 --> 00:54:56.660
but also a lot of shortcomings
of the public health system

00:54:56.660 --> 00:54:58.003
in the US and globally,

00:54:59.010 --> 00:55:04.010
what advice would you give
to aspiring and early stage

00:55:04.990 --> 00:55:07.140
public health professionals
and scientists?

00:55:08.880 --> 00:55:11.040
- It's a question I like
to deal with actually.

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:16.040
And first of all, be very
careful about a life plan.

00:55:16.770 --> 00:55:20.100
'Cause you can have no
idea what opportunities

00:55:20.100 --> 00:55:22.040
are going to be presented to you,

00:55:22.040 --> 00:55:24.360
you have no idea what the new problems

00:55:24.360 --> 00:55:26.780
are gonna be two years from now.

00:55:26.780 --> 00:55:31.050
So be careful about
solidifying a life plan.

00:55:31.050 --> 00:55:33.700
Number two, I tell the students

00:55:33.700 --> 00:55:35.320
that if you go into global health,

00:55:35.320 --> 00:55:37.433
you're not likely to get rich,

00:55:38.730 --> 00:55:40.883
but you're also not likely to get thanked.

00:55:41.762 --> 00:55:42.830
(Saad laughs)
But if you can get

00:55:42.830 --> 00:55:46.840
beyond those two things,
it's re very rewarding.

00:55:46.840 --> 00:55:50.690
And I tell them the
story of Pearl Kendrick,

00:55:50.690 --> 00:55:54.810
who worked for the Michigan
health department in the 1930s.

00:55:54.810 --> 00:55:59.740
And she developed pertussis
vaccine with another woman

00:55:59.740 --> 00:56:02.660
working in the Health Department.

00:56:02.660 --> 00:56:06.250
And when she died in 1980,

00:56:06.250 --> 00:56:09.080
the Dean of the School of Public Health

00:56:09.080 --> 00:56:11.930
at the University of
Michigan, Bill Remington,

00:56:11.930 --> 00:56:13.133
gave a eulogy.

00:56:14.000 --> 00:56:17.680
And he said, "There are
hundreds of thousands of people

00:56:17.680 --> 00:56:20.890
who are alive because of Pearl Kendrick."

00:56:20.890 --> 00:56:25.890
And he paused, and then he
asked, "Could you name one?"

00:56:25.970 --> 00:56:28.020
He said, "I can't either."

00:56:28.020 --> 00:56:30.703
And that's why she was
never adequately thanked.

00:56:31.750 --> 00:56:35.720
But he said, "She was
secure in the knowledge

00:56:35.720 --> 00:56:37.447
of what she was doing."

00:56:38.340 --> 00:56:42.130
And so I tell students,
you won't get rich,

00:56:42.130 --> 00:56:44.280
you won't be thanked, but be secure

00:56:44.280 --> 00:56:47.610
in the knowledge of what you are doing.

00:56:47.610 --> 00:56:51.190
And then I suggest to them that
in any program they go into,

00:56:51.190 --> 00:56:55.490
or any particular project
that they go into,

00:56:55.490 --> 00:56:58.810
to have three things in mind.

00:56:58.810 --> 00:57:01.810
Number one, the science.

00:57:01.810 --> 00:57:06.810
And Huxley defined science as simply,

00:57:07.620 --> 00:57:09.453
common sense at its best.

00:57:10.550 --> 00:57:13.810
So lots of people get afraid
of science these days,

00:57:13.810 --> 00:57:15.970
and you could see what's
happening in this country

00:57:15.970 --> 00:57:19.053
with coronavirus, people that
don't believe the sciences,

00:57:19.930 --> 00:57:22.550
but they all think they have common sense.

00:57:22.550 --> 00:57:26.900
And so if you define science
as common sense at its best,

00:57:26.900 --> 00:57:28.283
it makes it palatable.

00:57:29.340 --> 00:57:33.157
But number two, Will Duran said,

00:57:33.157 --> 00:57:37.917
"Add to the science
art, because," he said,

00:57:37.917 --> 00:57:42.910
"Then you have creative
common sense at its best."

00:57:42.910 --> 00:57:47.770
And he uses as his example
in Motap from Egypt,

00:57:47.770 --> 00:57:50.460
who was a physician and an artist,

00:57:50.460 --> 00:57:55.460
he designed the step pyramid
and then he had it built.

00:57:55.900 --> 00:58:00.250
So he says, "Adding art to your science

00:58:00.250 --> 00:58:02.580
gives you that creativity."

00:58:02.580 --> 00:58:06.050
But there's a third thing, and that is,

00:58:06.050 --> 00:58:07.963
to add a moral compass.

00:58:08.960 --> 00:58:13.960
And Roger Bacon is the example
I use from 700 years ago,

00:58:14.330 --> 00:58:19.330
when the Pope asked him
for a summary of science.

00:58:19.900 --> 00:58:23.550
And bacon worked a long
time on that summary.

00:58:23.550 --> 00:58:26.340
And then decided it's too long.

00:58:26.340 --> 00:58:28.520
And so he did a summary of
the summary, and finally,

00:58:28.520 --> 00:58:30.610
he did a summary of the
summary of the summary,

00:58:30.610 --> 00:58:33.100
that went to the Pope.

00:58:33.100 --> 00:58:36.040
Now I've not looked at this for decades,

00:58:36.040 --> 00:58:39.230
but there were three things
that came to mind for me.

00:58:39.230 --> 00:58:44.230
Number one, he was absolutely
enthusiastic about science.

00:58:44.370 --> 00:58:49.370
He predicted planes, submarines and cars.

00:58:51.290 --> 00:58:54.523
He predicted telescopes,
this is 700 years ago.

00:58:55.620 --> 00:58:58.270
So he was very excited about it.

00:58:58.270 --> 00:59:03.270
And number two, what I recall is that,

00:59:04.070 --> 00:59:07.470
after talking about the
possibilities of science,

00:59:07.470 --> 00:59:11.687
he said, "But science
has no moral compass."

00:59:12.680 --> 00:59:17.680
So you have to create
scientists with a moral compass.

00:59:17.680 --> 00:59:21.150
And the third thing, and
this is what makes me think

00:59:21.150 --> 00:59:23.290
the Pope never actually read it.

00:59:23.290 --> 00:59:27.630
He said, the church is
doing nothing to help them

00:59:27.630 --> 00:59:29.730
to provide a moral compass.

00:59:29.730 --> 00:59:34.640
So here we are still today,
get the science right, add art,

00:59:34.640 --> 00:59:37.680
get a moral compass, and put
those three things together,

00:59:37.680 --> 00:59:42.680
and now you have moral, creative,
common sense at its best.

00:59:42.730 --> 00:59:45.650
That's the shortest description I can make

00:59:45.650 --> 00:59:48.990
of what it should be like
to go into global health.

00:59:48.990 --> 00:59:50.220
- Thank you, really appreciate that.

00:59:50.220 --> 00:59:52.290
Thank you for joining us today.

00:59:52.290 --> 00:59:56.610
Thanks for all your passion,
wisdom and moral leadership

00:59:56.610 --> 00:59:59.013
in global health for many decades.

01:00:00.160 --> 01:00:03.310
Thank you to the audience
who joined us today.

01:00:03.310 --> 01:00:08.070
We are hoping to have our fall season

01:00:08.070 --> 01:00:11.590
for the conversation series
in-person, or at least hybrid.

01:00:11.590 --> 01:00:16.240
So stay tuned, we have a lot
of exciting guests lined up,

01:00:16.240 --> 01:00:18.640
and look forward to seeing you there.

01:00:18.640 --> 01:00:20.100
Even if we do it in-person,

01:00:20.100 --> 01:00:22.010
those of you who join
from around the country

01:00:22.010 --> 01:00:24.523
and from various parts of the world,

01:00:25.530 --> 01:00:27.950
you should be able to
follow us online as well

01:00:27.950 --> 01:00:28.810
even at that time.

01:00:28.810 --> 01:00:32.960
So thank you everyone,
and especially Dr. Foege.

01:00:32.960 --> 01:00:34.063
- Thank you Saad.

